course: biology
lesson: 300
title: The big biology quiz, part 3: dinosaurs, evolution, conservation and food
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: This is the last lesson of the course, so it gathers the big ideas once more. First: life changes over time. Over millions of years, living things that fit their world a little better have more young, and slowly new kinds appear, from early fish to dinosaurs, birds, wolves and us.
explain: Second: everything is connected. Wolves change how elk graze, bees turn flowers into fruit, and the worms and microbes in the soil feed the plants that feed us. Pull one thread and the whole web moves, like a spider's web when one strand is touched.
explain: Third: people are now the strongest force on the planet. People wiped out the dodo, but people also saved the bald eagle and brought wolves back to Yellowstone, so our choices decide which story comes next.
explain: Fourth: ask how we know. Fossils, tests and careful counting beat cartoons and myths, from the Flintstones riding dinosaurs to elephants scared of mice.
explain: And last: every child can help, with a wild corner of the garden, a bird feeder, a bit of litter picked up and a note of what lives nearby.
fact: The dinosaurs, apart from birds, died out about 66 million years ago when an asteroid hit what is now Mexico; dust and darkness killed plants and then the animals that ate them, but birds survived, so birds are living dinosaurs.
fact: People and T. rex never lived together; about 66 million years separate them, and the oldest human ancestors are only a few million years old, and Stegosaurus died out longer before T. rex lived than T. rex lived before you.
fact: Giraffes with slightly longer necks reached more food and won more fights, so they had more young, and over many generations necks grew longer; this is natural selection, and stretching your neck does not change what you pass on.
fact: A fossil is the remains or traces of a living thing kept in rock, such as bones, shells, footprints or even dung, and most fossils formed under water.
fact: The dodo of Mauritius could not fly and had no fear of people; it was wiped out in the 1600s by people and by the rats, pigs and other animals they brought, which ate its eggs.
fact: Wolves were brought back to Yellowstone in 1995 and changed where and how elk grazed, and some trees grew back along rivers; scientists still argue about how big this ripple was, but a top predator can shape a whole food web.
fact: The bald eagle came back after the pesticide DDT, which made its eggshells thin, was banned in the United States in 1972 and the birds were protected; a book called Silent Spring by Rachel Carson had warned about DDT.
fact: A tomato grows from a flower and holds seeds, so botanists call it a fruit, while cooks call it a vegetable; a potato is a swollen stem, a carrot is a root and broccoli is a bunch of flower buds.
fact: Dogs come from wolves tamed more than 15000 years ago and were the first tame animals, while cats came later to farming villages to hunt mice and were honoured as sacred in ancient Egypt.
fact: Elephants fearing mice is a myth; they are startled by sudden movement and wary of bees, and other myths are wrong too: ostriches do not bury their heads, lemmings do not jump and goldfish remember for months.
fact: Bees and other pollinators carry pollen between flowers so fruit and seeds can form, and about a third of our food depends on pollinators; wild bees need flowers and fewer poisons.
fact: A child can help nature by leaving a wild corner, picking up litter safely, feeding birds, recording sightings for scientists and wasting less, and small acts by many people add up.
exercise: What killed the dinosaurs? | An asteroid that hit Mexico about 66 million years ago | A huge flood that covered the whole Earth for forty years | Early people who hunted them all | A long war between the meat-eating and plant-eating dinosaurs
exercise: Which dinosaurs are still alive today? | Birds | Crocodiles | Komodo dragons | None at all
exercise: Did people and T. rex ever live at the same time? | No, about 66 million years separate them | Yes, early people hunted T. rex with spears | Yes, but only in caves | Yes, people rode them like horses and kept them in pens
exercise: Why do giraffes have long necks? | Longer-necked giraffes got more food and had more young, over many generations | Each giraffe stretched its neck for leaves and passed the stretch on to its young | A giraffe's neck keeps growing all its life | Giraffes were made long by hanging from trees
exercise: What is this slow change through who has more young called? | Natural selection | Hibernation | Pollination by insects | Camouflage in the wild
exercise: What is a fossil? | The remains or traces of a living thing kept in rock | A living animal frozen in ice | A stone carved by people long ago to look like an animal | A bone from an animal that died last year
exercise: Which of these can be a fossil? | A footprint or even dung | Only a whole skeleton with every bone | Only a shell | Only a tooth from a shark or a dinosaur
exercise: What happened to the dodo? | People and the animals they brought wiped it out in the 1600s | It flew away to another island where nobody could ever find it again | It turned into the pigeon over time | It is still hiding in Mauritius
exercise: What did wolves change after they came back to Yellowstone in 1995? | Where and how the elk grazed | The colour of the rivers in the park | The weather in the park | Nothing at all, since wolves only eat
exercise: Why did the bald eagle come back? | DDT was banned and eagles were protected | Eagles learned to eat plastic and grew strong | People fed every single eagle by hand | Eagles moved in from Africa
exercise: Which book warned about the dangers of DDT? | Silent Spring | The Jungle Book | Charlotte's Web | Winnie-the-Pooh
exercise: Is a tomato a fruit? | Yes, botanists call it a fruit because it holds seeds | No, it is a root that grows under the ground, like a carrot | No, it is a stem like a potato | No, it is a kind of nut
exercise: Where did dogs come from? | From wolves tamed more than 15000 years ago | From foxes tamed by the Egyptians beside the Nile | From wild cats in farming villages | From hyenas in Africa
exercise: Are elephants afraid of mice? | No, it is a myth; they are startled by sudden movement | Yes, more than of any other animal on Earth | Yes, mice climb up into their trunks and block them so they cannot breathe | Only baby elephants are
exercise: Why do bees matter for our food? | They pollinate flowers so fruit and seeds can form | They make all the sugar we eat | They eat the pests on every farm and garden in the land | They plant seeds with their stings
exercise: What can a child do to help nature? | Leave a wild corner, pick up litter safely and record sightings | Catch wild animals to keep as pets, so they are safe from harm at home | Pick wild flowers to take home | Nothing, only scientists can help
test: In The Flintstones, Fred works with a dinosaur at the quarry. What is wrong? | People and dinosaurs missed each other by about 66 million years | Dinosaurs were far too lazy to do any work for people | Dinosaurs lived only in the sea | Nothing, people and dinosaurs really lived and worked together in quarries
test: Who lived closer in time to T. rex: a Stegosaurus or you? | You did | Stegosaurus did | Both lived at exactly the same time | Neither, T. rex lived after both
test: «my dog's basically a wolf on a sofa» Is there truth in it? | Yes, dogs come from wolves tamed more than 15000 years ago | No, dogs come from cats | No, dogs come from bears that were tamed | Yes, every dog is still a wild wolf that could go back to the forest
test: «giraffe stretched its neck reaching for leaves, so the baby got a long neck too» What is the best correction? | Stretching is not passed on; longer-necked giraffes just had more young | Right, every stretch goes to the babies | Right, but only if the mother stretches | Wrong, giraffe necks are short at birth and never grow
test: In Ice Age a family of silly dodos fights over three melons. Why could real dodos not escape the people who came to Mauritius? | They could not fly and had no fear of people | They were too fast to be caught | They lived only at the top of mountains | They were already gone 66 million years before
test: Count them: a wolf pack of 6 runs through Yellowstone. How many tails and how many ears? | 6 tails and 12 ears | 12 tails and 12 ears | 6 tails and 6 ears | 6 tails and 24 ears
test: Count them: in Dumbo 3 elephants jump onto a box away from the mouse. How many trunks, tails and legs? | 3 trunks, 3 tails and 12 legs | 3 trunks, 6 tails and 12 legs | 6 trunks, 3 tails and 12 legs | 3 trunks, 3 tails and 6 legs
test: What would happen to a meadow if all its bees vanished? | Many flowers would make no fruit or seeds, and fewer plants would grow back | Flowers would grow much bigger without bees taking their nectar every single day | Nothing, flowers do not need bees | The grass would turn into flowers
test: «tomato's a veg, my mum said, end of story» What is the best answer? | Cooks call it a vegetable, but botanists call it a fruit, as it holds seeds | Your mum is wrong, it is a root that grows under the soil like a carrot | It is a nut, actually | It is neither, it is a mushroom
test: «took the wolves out of the park ages ago and now they put them back, why bother» What did bringing them back show? | A top predator can change how other animals and even plants live | Wolves eat all the trees | Nothing changes when a predator returns to its old home | Elk need wolves to show them where to find the best food
test: A spray kills insects on a farm but makes the eggshells of birds thin. What would happen to birds like eagles? | Fewer chicks would hatch, and their numbers would fall | Eagles would grow stronger shells | Nothing, eagles do not eat anything sprayed | More eagles would hatch than before
test: «what's the point, I'm just a kid, can't save the planet» What is the best reply? | Small acts like a wild corner or recording sightings add up when many do them | You are right, children cannot help, so it is best to leave it to adults | Only famous people can help nature | Helping nature is only for zoos
exam: Identify the principal cause of the extinction of the non-avian dinosaurs. | The impact of an asteroid upon the region now known as Mexico, approximately sixty-six million years ago | The hunting of dinosaurs by early human beings | A universal flood covering the whole of the Earth | Prolonged conflict between the carnivorous and herbivorous dinosaur species across all the continents
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the elongation of the neck of the giraffe. | Individuals with longer necks obtained more food and produced more offspring over successive generations | Each individual stretched its neck and transmitted the stretching to its offspring | The neck of each giraffe continues to elongate throughout its life | The elongation was produced by suspension from the branches of trees
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the recovery of the bald eagle? | It followed the prohibition of the pesticide DDT and the legal protection of the species | It followed the provision of artificial food to every individual by the authorities | It followed the arrival of eagles from Africa | It followed the adaptation of eagles to the consumption of plastic
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a tomato is classified by botanists as a fruit. | It develops from a flower and contains seeds | It is grown beneath the surface of the soil | It is consumed principally in a sweet dish | It is a modified stem of the plant
